175
00:09:10,690 --> 00:09:13,550
从而 构成了生物体的形状
and,thus,in the shape of the organisms.

176
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:16,120
这就是为什么
Which is why

177
00:09:16,230 --> 00:09:18,420
要了解外星人的模样
to know what aliens look like,

178
00:09:18,530 --> 00:09:22,120
我们就必须更多地了解它们生存的星球
we must learn more about the planets they live on.

179
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:23,490
一直到最近
Until very recently,

180
00:09:23,610 --> 00:09:26,060
我们都没有其它行星存在的证据
we had no proof other planets existed,

181
00:09:26,170 --> 00:09:29,600
更别说它们的地貌及大气层
let alone any idea what their landscapes or atmospheres

182
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:31,430
会是何种模样
might be like.

183
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:35,000
然而现在 作为人类史上首次
But now,for the first time in human history,

184
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:39,810
我们能够看到远在我们太阳系以外的世界了
we can see worlds far outside our solar system.

185
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:42,710
现在我们知道了E.T.（外星人）可能居住的地方
And now that we know where E.T.s could live,

186
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:46,350
我们越发接近揭露它们隐匿的面孔了
we're getting closer to revealing their hidden faces.

187
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:51,730
我们若想知道外星人的模样
lf we want to know what aliens look like,

188
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:55,160
我们首先得知道它们居住之地的一些情况
we first have to know something about the places they live.

189
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:59,100
直到最近 这是不可能的
Until recently,this was impossible.

190
00:09:59,210 --> 00:10:01,700
我们的望远镜只能观看恒星
Our telescopes could only see stars,

191
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:04,070
看不到其周围的行星
not the planets that orbit them.

192
00:10:04,190 --> 00:10:08,110
如今 外星人猎手已有了专门的研究飞船
Today,alien hunters have a dedicated research ship

193
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:11,380
漂浮在距离地球2000万英里的地方
floating 20 million miles from Earth,

194
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:15,860
它发现的新世界数以千计
and it's discovering new worlds by the thousand.

195
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:21,580
3…2…
3...2...

196
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:22,700
点火
Engines start.

197
00:10:22,810 --> 00:10:24,160
起飞
Zero.

198
00:10:24,270 --> 00:10:27,400
德尔塔Ⅱ载着开普勒升空了
And liftoff of the Delta ll rocket with Kepler.

199
00:10:27,510 --> 00:10:29,600
2009年
ln 2009,

200
00:10:29,710 --> 00:10:33,070
航天局发射了其最新型太空望远镜 -
NASA launched its latest space telescope --

201
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:34,640
开普勒
Kepler.

202
00:10:34,750 --> 00:10:37,540
其目的不是为了拍照
lt's designed not to take pictures,

203
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:39,380
而是侦测遥远恒星亮度上
but to detect the tiniest changes

204
00:10:39,490 --> 00:10:41,810
细微的变化
in the brightness of distant stars.

205
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:46,020
其目标是银河系我们所在的旋臂上的一片区域
lts target area is a patch of our arm of the Milky Way

206
00:10:46,130 --> 00:10:49,790
从我们这里一直延伸出去有3000光年远
stretching out 3,000 light-years away from us.

207
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:52,320
哈佛大学教授迪米塔.萨瑟罗夫
Harvard professor Dimitar Sasselov

208
00:10:52,430 --> 00:10:55,090
是开普勒首席科学家之一
is one of Kepler's lead scientists.

209
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:57,830
用开普勒望远镜
The beauty of how the Kepler telescope

210
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:01,230
探索小如地球的行星的方法
discovers planets as small as the earth is the method,

211
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:03,030
我们称其为凌日法
which we call the transit method.

212
00:11:03,150 --> 00:11:04,330
这很容易理解
lt's very easy to understand.

213
00:11:04,450 --> 00:11:07,670
就是 行星在其轨道上
So,the planet is passing on its orbit

214
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:09,940
在恒星的面前经过
in front of the star.

215
00:11:10,050 --> 00:11:14,010
它的阴影造成星光有一点点昏暗
lts shadow causes that light to dip just a little bit,

216
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:15,980
这样一来我们就知道有行星的存在了
and that's how we know there is a planet there.

217
00:11:16,090 --> 00:11:19,450
到开普勒完成其使命时
By the time Kepler is done with its mission,

218
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:21,480
迪米塔预计它将会发现
Dimitar expects it will have found

219
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:24,950
约100颗地球大小的行星
around 100 planets the size of Earth.

220
00:11:25,070 --> 00:11:28,190
然而它所寻找的大量的行星
But the vast majority of the planets it is finding

221
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:31,890
几乎与我们的世界毫无共同之处
have almost nothing in common with our world.

222
00:11:32,010 --> 00:11:34,030
开普勒已经有了一八宝箱的怪异行星
Kepler already has a treasure chest

223
00:11:34,140 --> 00:11:35,630
如果你愿意的话 -
of weird planets,if you will --

224
00:11:35,740 --> 00:11:38,100
非常有趣的 各种各样的行星
very interesting,diverse planets.

225
00:11:38,210 --> 00:11:42,440
我们有开普勒 - 10号星 它坚如钢铁
So,we have Kepler-10,which is as hard as iron.

226
00:11:42,550 --> 00:11:44,310
接着我们有两三颗行星
Then we have two or three planets

227
00:11:44,420 --> 00:11:46,940
在六颗行星组成的开普勒 - 11星系里
in the Kepler-11 system of six.

228
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:48,980
其中的一两颗是水行星
One or two of them are water planets --

229
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:51,780
一片汪洋
endless ocean.

230
00:11:51,890 --> 00:11:53,780
接下来我们的行星
Then we have planets

231
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:58,320
几乎是浓密的大充气球或发泡塑料
almost the density of a beach ball or styrofoam.

232
00:11:58,430 --> 00:12:03,430
或许最有趣的开普勒发现
Perhaps the most intriguing of Kepler's discoveries

233
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:07,630
是约300倍的超大尺寸版本的地球 -
are around 300 super-sized versions of Earth --

234
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:12,310
岩石构成的行星 但要重上5倍
planets made of rock,but up to five times as heavy.

235
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:23,880
要说有人能想象其景色
lf anyone can imagine the landscapes

236
00:12:23,990 --> 00:12:28,420
外星人会在那里慢跑 游泳 骑行
where aliens might jog,swim,or glide,

237
00:12:28,530 --> 00:12:31,150
那就是戴安娜.瓦伦西亚
it's Diana Valencia.

238
00:12:31,270 --> 00:12:32,790
业余三项全能运动员
Part-time triathlete,

239
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:34,820
她是首批剖析这些超级地球的
she's one of the first geologists

240
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:38,730
地质学家之一
to break ground on these super earths.

241
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:40,830
我没有锤子
l do not have a hammer.

242
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:42,260
我不破碎石头
l do not break up rocks.

243
00:12:42,380 --> 00:12:44,030
我所做的是建立数学模型
What l do is l do numerical models

244
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:45,740
来了解地球的运作
to understand how the Earth works

245
00:12:45,850 --> 00:12:47,800
并用它来了解
and use that to understand

246
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,840
更大或更小的行星是如何运作的
how bigger earths and similar planets work,as well.

247
00:12:51,950 --> 00:12:53,010
为了了解
To understand

248
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:55,540
超级地球是否能够存在生命
whether the super earths could harbor life,

249
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:59,620
戴安娜瞄准推动岩石行星的
Diana is zeroing in on the basic geological engine

250
00:12:59,730 --> 00:13:01,420
基本地质引擎 -
that powers rocky planets --

251
00:13:01,530 --> 00:13:04,150
板块构造
plate tectonics.

252
00:13:04,270 --> 00:13:07,060
行星坚硬外壳运动的
The movement of a planet's hard outer crust

253
00:13:07,170 --> 00:13:09,560
推手是其底下
is driven by a hot and viscous layer

254
00:13:09,670 --> 00:13:11,960
高温粘稠的半融化岩石
of semi-molten rock below it

255
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:15,530
其活动宛如一罐冒泡的蜂蜜
moving much like a jar of bubbling honey.

256
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:21,100
这个实验向我们表现了大致的情况
This experiment here shows us in broad lines what happens.

257
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:24,040
地幔是一种非常粘稠的液体
The mantle is a very viscous fluid,

258
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:27,210
两种液体对温度都很敏感
and both fluids are very sensitive to temperature.

259
00:13:27,320 --> 00:13:32,420
我们打开这个来加热模拟地球的引擎
So,as we turn this heat up to simulate Earth's engine,

260
00:13:32,530 --> 00:13:37,120
你就会看到在其表面以下的活动
you will start seeing motion underneath the surface.

261
00:13:37,230 --> 00:13:39,320
现在你看到了翻滚
Now you see the overturn.

262
00:13:39,430 --> 00:13:40,990
现在你看到物体
Now you start seeing things

263
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:42,760
往各个方向活动
that are moving all sorts of directions.

264
00:13:42,870 --> 00:13:44,430
不只是向上运动
lt's not just moving up.

265
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:48,530
随着温度的提升
As heat rises,

266
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:51,570
它在地幔中形成了对流体
it forms convective cells in the mantle,

267
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:55,340
这引发了表面板块的移动
which cause the plates on the surface to shift.

268
00:13:55,450 --> 00:13:59,910
这些移动触发了火山喷发和地震 -
These shifts trigger volcanic eruptions and earthquakes --

269
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:03,420
与我们生死攸关的事件
events we associate more with death than life.

270
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:09,320
但这只是短暂的情形
But that's just the short-term view.

271
00:14:09,430 --> 00:14:12,020
从戴安娜的地质学角度
From Diana's geological perspective,

272
00:14:12,130 --> 00:14:15,620
此种从我们星球内部到大气层的物质循环
this cycling of material from the inside of our planet

273
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:20,800
对生命的进化一直是至关重要的
to the atmosphere has been vital to the evolution of life.

274
00:14:20,910 --> 00:14:22,970
多亏了这个过程
Thanks to this process,

275
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:24,730
地球的表面温度
the surface temperature of the Earth

276
00:14:24,850 --> 00:14:26,100
没有摇摆得很厉害
has not swung very much,

277
00:14:26,210 --> 00:14:28,600
液态水一直围绕着它
and it has been around that of liquid water

278
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:30,180
长达数十亿年
for over billions of years.

279
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:38,610
超级地球更大
Super earths are bigger

280
00:14:38,730 --> 00:14:41,020
所以内部更热
and therefore hotter on the inside.

281
00:14:41,130 --> 00:14:43,890
你开大热量后
And when you turn up the heat,

282
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,160
板块构造就加大了马力
plate tectonics kicks into a higher gear.

283
00:14:47,270 --> 00:14:51,140
那或许就意味着更多的火山喷发 更多的地震
That may mean more volcanoes and more earthquakes.

284
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:58,300
但同时 星球有了更加稳定的温度
But also,a planet with a much more stable temperature.

285
00:14:58,410 --> 00:14:59,870
在超级地球上
On super earths,

286
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:02,740
因为对流要快得多
because convection would be much faster,

287
00:15:02,850 --> 00:15:05,370
这种循环可能影响也快得多 -
this cycle could respond much quicker --

288
00:15:05,490 --> 00:15:07,910
或许是数量级的加快
perhaps an order of magnitude quicker.

289
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:09,450
于是我们就可以推测
And then we can speculate

290
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:12,550
那已经具有了复杂生命进化的可能
that that has enabled the evolution of complex life.

291
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:14,950
来想想超级地球
Think about how a super earth

292
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:17,290
将会如何应对抹去了
would have dealt with the impact

293
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:20,390
恐龙的陨星的撞击的
of the meteorite that wiped out the dinosaurs.

294
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:24,390
在地球上 这个事件引发了全球性严冬
On Earth,this event triggered an extended global winter

295
00:15:24,510 --> 00:15:28,160
招致了那些冷血巨无霸的灭亡
that spelled the demise of those cold-blooded giants.

296
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:30,700
然而在更大的星球上
But on a bigger planet,

297
00:15:30,810 --> 00:15:33,200
能够更好地控制其温度
better able to control its temperature,

298
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:36,410
恐龙或许能幸免于难
dinosaurs might survive

299
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:40,640
有机会进化出更大的大脑
and have the chance to evolve bigger brains.

300
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:44,010
但是 生活在巨大版本的地球上
However,there is one major downside

301
00:15:44,130 --> 00:15:46,780
有一个严重的缺陷
to living on a giant version of Earth.

302
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:51,590
我们地球的核心是一个旋转的液态金属球
The core of our world is a spinning ball of liquid metal

303
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:54,390
产生出强大的磁场
generating a powerful magnetic field.

304
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:59,190
该磁场偏转了从太阳发出的危险辐射流
That field deflects a torrent of dangerous radiation from the Sun

305
00:15:59,310 --> 00:16:03,830
构成了一个对这里的所有生命的保护盾
and forms a protective cocoon for all life here.

306
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:06,140
戴安娜的模型预测
Diana's models predict

307
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:10,310
超级地球或许就没有这样的力场
that super earths may not have these force fields.

308
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:11,670
极有可能
lt's very possible

309
00:16:11,790 --> 00:16:13,640
这些行星并没有熔核
that these planets do not have a molten core,

310
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:17,010
因为其内部压力太大
because their interiors are under so much pressure.

311
00:16:17,130 --> 00:16:19,980
因此 你如果作为生物
So,if you are a creature in a planet

312
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,380
在一颗没有磁场的行星上
that doesn't have a geomagnetic field,

313
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:25,350
就会一直遭受高能粒子的轰击
you are being bombarded by high-energy particles,

314
00:16:25,470 --> 00:16:27,160
它们会与你的细胞发生作用
and those are interacting with your cells,

315
00:16:27,270 --> 00:16:28,720
引发突变 大概这样
causing mutations,probably.

316
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:32,700
因此 你就得更加机灵 作为一个有机体
So,you have to be clever,as an organism,

317
00:16:32,810 --> 00:16:34,430
去适应这样的环境
to adapt to those conditions.

318
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:37,400
何种外星人能够存活在
What kind of alien could survive

319
00:16:37,510 --> 00:16:40,340
浸泡在辐射里的超级地球上呢？
on a radiation-soaked super earth?

320
00:16:40,450 --> 00:16:43,140
它将需要有一个保护壳
lt would need a protective shell,

321
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:46,280
或许搀和了铅之类的重金属
perhaps laced with heavy metals like lead.

322
00:16:46,390 --> 00:16:48,910
它将会有强大的肢体和锋利的爪子
lt would have powerful limbs and sharp claws

323
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:50,980
使其在强烈的辐射爆发期间
to let it burrow under the ground

324
00:16:51,090 --> 00:16:52,720
钻入底下
during heavy radiation bursts.

325
00:16:52,830 --> 00:16:54,980
最为重要的是 它将需要
Most important,it would need

326
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:57,720
有效的基因修复机制
effective genetic repair mechanisms

327
00:16:57,830 --> 00:17:02,190
来修复对其细胞不可避免的辐射伤害
to fix the inevitable radiation damage to its cells.

328
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:04,930
纯属幻想？ 或许不是
Pure fantasy? Maybe not.

329
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:07,460
类似的生命形式
Similar life-forms,

330
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:10,830
虽然小许多 叫做水熊虫
albeit much smaller,called water bears,

331
00:17:10,950 --> 00:17:12,870
存活在地球上滚烫的高辐射地区
survive in boiling-hot,

332
00:17:12,980 --> 00:17:16,070
岩质超级地球的居民
radiation-blasted regions on Earth.

333
00:17:16,180 --> 00:17:18,570
有可能看上去
lnhabitants of rocky super earths

334
00:17:18,690 --> 00:17:21,380
惊人的类似
might look surprisingly familiar.

335
00:17:21,490 --> 00:17:25,050
然而来想象一下一个没有岩石的世界里
But imagine a world where there is no rock,

336
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:28,220
生物生活在海洋里
and where creatures living in the ocean

337
00:17:28,330 --> 00:17:31,730
以及飞翔在天空中
also fly through the sky.

338
00:17:35,630 --> 00:17:37,350
地球上
On Earth,

339
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:41,850
进化产生了生命的千变万化 -
evolution has produced countless variations on life --

340
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,950
动物潜行水中
animals that glide through the water

341
00:17:45,060 --> 00:17:47,350
翱翔天空
and soar through the sky.

342
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:52,550
芸芸众生或滑 或爬 或走 或跑
Beings that slither,crawl,walk,and run.

343
00:17:52,670 --> 00:17:54,590
如果其它星球上的生命
lf life on other worlds

344
00:17:54,700 --> 00:17:58,560
遵循我们这里的生命进化模式
follows the evolutionary pattern of life here,

345
00:17:58,670 --> 00:18:03,130
会有其它什么令人费解的特色出现呢？
what other mind-bending features might arise?

346
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:06,640
好 你有了一颗行星 有了大气层
Okay. So,you got the planet,you've got the atmosphere.

347
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:07,760
夸张的
Exaggerated.

348
00:18:07,870 --> 00:18:09,740
对
Yeah.

349
00:18:09,850 --> 00:18:11,510
在剑桥市麻省理工学院
At M.l.T. in Cambridge,

350
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:15,940
天体物理学家萨拉.西格和生物化学家威廉.贝恩斯
astrophysicist Sara Seager and biochemist William Bains

351
00:18:16,060 --> 00:18:17,450
开始想象
are beginning to imagine

352
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:19,980
这些遥远的星球会是何种模样
what these distant worlds will be like.

353
00:18:20,090 --> 00:18:22,550
大气会出自某处
The atmosphere's gonna come from somewhere,

354
00:18:22,660 --> 00:18:25,550
这样你就有了火山不断产生出大气
so you're gonna have volcanoes producing atmosphere.

355
00:18:25,670 --> 00:18:29,860
他们试图预测行星的大小与成分将如何
They're trying to predict how a planet's size and composition

356
00:18:29,970 --> 00:18:31,760
塑造其生物圈的
will shape its biosphere.

357
00:18:31,870 --> 00:18:34,800
在发现日外行星之前
Before the discovery of ex oplanets,

358
00:18:34,910 --> 00:18:36,160
人们以为所有的行星系
people thought that all planetary systems

359
00:18:36,270 --> 00:18:37,270
都与我们太阳系相像
would be like our solar system.

360
00:18:37,380 --> 00:18:38,570
从那时起
And since that time,

361
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:42,100
日外行星与日外行星系的发现
discoveries of ex oplanets and ex oplanetary systems

362
00:18:42,220 --> 00:18:45,270
让我们一次又一次的惊讶
have surprised us over and over and over again.

363
00:18:45,390 --> 00:18:48,140
那么 有啥变化呢？ 通通变了
So,what has changed? Everything has changed.

364
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:49,910
大多数科幻小说假设
Most science fiction assumes

365
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:51,780
外星人会四处奔走
that aliens are gonna be walking around,

366
00:18:51,890 --> 00:18:53,250
它们要呼吸空气
they're gonna be breathing air.

367
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:54,820
你知道的 它们登上飞船
You know,they landed a starship,

368
00:18:54,930 --> 00:18:56,620
它们与船长共餐
and they shared dinner with the captain.

369
00:18:56,730 --> 00:18:59,450
你看某些行星环境
You look at some of the planetary environments

370
00:18:59,570 --> 00:19:00,660
外面的
out there,

371
00:19:00,770 --> 00:19:02,220
那是不会发生的
and that is not gonna happen.

372
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:03,960
那将会是截然不同的
lt's gonna be very different.

373
00:19:04,070 --> 00:19:07,260
最近 萨拉和威廉
Recently,Sara and William

374
00:19:07,370 --> 00:19:11,070
一直在研究GJ 1214b
have been studying GJ 1214B,

375
00:19:11,180 --> 00:19:13,270
一颗40光年外的行星
a planet about 40 light-years away

376
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:15,800
是地球大小的两倍还多
that's more than twice the size of Earth

377
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:19,270
显示出有大气层的迹象
and shows signs of having an atmosphere.

378
00:19:19,390 --> 00:19:21,310
他俩正致力于发现
Together they are working to discover

379
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:23,480
1214b云层下方
what it might be like

380
00:19:23,590 --> 00:19:26,480
会是何种情况
to descend beneath the clouds of 1214B.

381
00:19:26,590 --> 00:19:28,350
现在 这个星球 -
Now,this planet --

382
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:29,980
我们无法完全肯定其成分是什么
we're not totally sure what it's made of,

383
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:33,350
但它有可能是颗水行星 有水蒸气
but it could be a water planet with a steam atmosphere.

384
00:19:33,470 --> 00:19:35,960
根据该行星的温度
And depending on the temperature of the planet,

385
00:19:36,070 --> 00:19:38,190
其液态水与具有水蒸气之间的
the clean division between liquid water

386
00:19:38,310 --> 00:19:42,630
清晰界限可能并不存在
and air with water vapor in it may not exist.

387
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:45,600
有何种生命可能会出现在
What sort of life could possibly emerge

388
00:19:45,710 --> 00:19:49,270
一个滚烫的蒸汽世界里呢？
on a boiling-hot,steam world?

389
00:19:49,380 --> 00:19:51,110
这个 在地球上
So,on Earth,

390
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:54,110
此种具有开水和蒸汽的环境
an environment like this with boiling water and steam

391
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:56,480
会加害于几乎所有的生命
is inimicable to nearly all life.

392
00:19:56,590 --> 00:19:58,580
但是我们正努力去想象一个外星世界
But we're trying to imagine an alien world

393
00:19:58,690 --> 00:20:00,050
在那里 这就是正常的环境
in which this is the normal environment,

394
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:03,050
我们现在就能够开始模拟一个星球
and we can now start to model a planet

395
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:05,320
它一片汪洋
that has a huge ocean covering it

396
00:20:05,430 --> 00:20:07,560
然而却是热得要命
and nevertheless is incredibly hot.

397
00:20:07,670 --> 00:20:09,620
那就让我们去思索
That makes us think about,

398
00:20:09,740 --> 00:20:11,660
海洋里会有生命存在么？
"Could there be life in the ocean?

399
00:20:11,770 --> 00:20:13,590
能有化学作用么？
Can the chemistry work?

400
00:20:13,710 --> 00:20:15,560
"如果能 会是何种样子呢？
And if it can,what would it look like?"

401
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:22,000
DNA这样的分子不会存活在这样的环境中的
A molecule like DNA wouldn't survive these conditions,

402
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:24,340
但威廉认为
but William believes

403
00:20:24,450 --> 00:20:26,740
更为耐热的遗传物质
more heat-tolerant genetic material

404
00:20:26,850 --> 00:20:28,910
将有可能进化
would likely evolve.

405
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:31,280
他开始想象
And he's beginning to imagine

406
00:20:31,390 --> 00:20:33,910
能量有可能填写
what entries might fill the pages of a book of life

407
00:20:34,030 --> 00:20:37,020
GJ1214b的生命之书
for GJ1214B.

408
00:20:37,130 --> 00:20:40,760
这颗行星的大气层主要是水
The atmosphere of this planet would be mostly water.

409
00:20:40,870 --> 00:20:42,920
那就是水蒸气
lt would be steam.

410
00:20:43,040 --> 00:20:45,730
它会非常密集 温度很高
lt would be very dense and be very hot.

411
00:20:45,840 --> 00:20:47,830
因此 你一路往下
So,as you go down through it,

412
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:51,370
你会看到漂浮的植物 会飞的植物生命
you'll find drifting plants,flying plant life,

413
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:54,900
有一系列生物食用那些植物生命
and a whole range of organisms that eats that plant life.

414
00:20:55,020 --> 00:20:57,710
生物就像是飞鱼
Organisms would be sort of flying fish

415
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:00,210
或水鸟 这取决于你怎么看待它了
or swimming birds,depending on how you look at it.

416
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:03,340
它们能够翱翔
So,they'll be able to actually fly through

417
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:08,620
或畅游在这个区域里 这里仿佛一片大海
or swim through this zone almost as if it was ocean.

418
00:21:08,730 --> 00:21:13,620
地球的海洋产生出了各种生物
Earth's oceans gave rise to creatures of all sizes,

419
00:21:13,730 --> 00:21:17,730
然而深海之王是巨大的滤食动物 -
but the kings of the deep are the giant filter feeders --

420
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:19,830
鲸鱼
whales.

421
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:23,130
1214b可能也有它们
1214B could have them,too.

422
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:25,170
因此 我们在这里想象生物
So,the organism we're imagining here

423
00:21:25,280 --> 00:21:26,700
以非常类似的方式活动
works in a very similar way.

424
00:21:26,810 --> 00:21:29,100
它或许具有非常不同的形状
lt might have a very different shape.

425
00:21:29,220 --> 00:21:31,870
然而 它活动在大海里
But it moves through the ocean

426
00:21:31,990 --> 00:21:34,580
然后能够向上进入这个界面区
and then can move up into this interfacial zone.

427
00:21:34,690 --> 00:21:37,110
它们能够长时间呆在界面区里
They can spend much longer in the interfacial zone

428
00:21:37,220 --> 00:21:40,780
行动至很高的地方 就是说 鲸跃
and move much further up into it than,say,a whale breaching

429
00:21:40,890 --> 00:21:43,120
因为密度较大
because the density is greater.

430
00:21:43,230 --> 00:21:45,590
这个水世界是
This aquatic world is a vision

431
00:21:45,700 --> 00:21:47,720
地球可能一直所喜欢的愿景
of what Earth might have been like

432
00:21:47,830 --> 00:21:50,390
虽然它更大 水更多
if it were larger and wetter.

433
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:55,400
人类无法生存在此地 但生命能找到生路么？
Humans couldn't survive here,but could life find a way?

434
00:21:55,510 --> 00:21:58,670
我们尚…不知道
We don't know...yet.

435
00:21:58,780 --> 00:22:00,940
科学中有许多重要的东西
There are many important things in science,

436
00:22:01,050 --> 00:22:03,500
其中最为重要的一个是想象力
and one of the most important ones is imagination.

437
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:05,570
因此 至今让人着迷不已的是 -
So,what is so fascinating so far --

438
00:22:05,690 --> 00:22:07,940
在日外行星上 物理和化学规律中
in ex oplanets,anything is possible

439
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:10,110
一切皆有可能
within the laws of physics and chemistry,

440
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:13,680
我们想象的任何东西都将存在于某处
and anything we imagine will exist somewhere.

441
00:22:13,790 --> 00:22:17,890
出现了水 于是你就能找到生命
Follow the water. There,you'll find life.

442
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,690
那就是天体生物学家想说的话
That's what the astrobiologists like to say.

443
00:22:21,800 --> 00:22:24,530
然而若是没有水呢？
But what if there is no water?

444
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:27,330
若是行星笼罩在毒气里
What about planets enveloped in to xic air

445
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:29,660
生命的基本构件
where the building blocks of life

446
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:34,170
是不是完全不同于我们这里呢？
are completely different from our own?

447
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:36,840
它们也能够存活么？
Could they also be alive?

448
00:22:38,040 --> 00:22:41,170
生命是顽强的
Life is tenacious.

449
00:22:42,140 --> 00:22:43,630
地球上的角角落落
Everywhere on Earth,

450
00:22:43,750 --> 00:22:46,400
从最冷的大海深处
from the coldest depths of the sea

451
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:49,440
到沸腾的火山裂隙
to the boiling fissures of volcanoes,

452
00:22:49,550 --> 00:22:52,310
生命都能找到繁衍之道
living things find a way to thrive.

453
00:22:52,420 --> 00:22:55,380
不过外行星的条件
But the conditions on alien planets

454
00:22:55,490 --> 00:22:57,610
可能更极端
could be even more extreme.

455
00:22:57,730 --> 00:23:01,950
我们发现了火与冰的世界
We're discovering worlds of fire and ice,

456
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:04,820
永久黑夜的世界
worlds of permanent night,

457
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:09,290
狂风大作 经久不息的世界
worlds where hurricanes are constant and global.

458
00:23:09,410 --> 00:23:13,140
何种外星人能够生活在这些地狱般的地方呢？
What kind of alien could live in these hellish places?

459
00:23:16,750 --> 00:23:21,040
格利泽581d漂浮在距离地球20光年远的
Gliese 581 D floats 20 light-years away from Earth

460
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:23,710
天秤座里
in the constellation Libra.

461
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:27,140
它是我们发现有可能有外星生命的
lt's one of the small group of planets we have spotted

462
00:23:27,260 --> 00:23:30,180
少量行星中的一颗
that might harbor alien life.

463
00:23:30,290 --> 00:23:33,550
其红色母星发出的热量
lts red star burns

464
00:23:33,660 --> 00:23:36,450
只有我们太阳的一半
with only half the heat of our Sun,

465
00:23:36,570 --> 00:23:39,560
但因为该行星旋转得很慢
but because the planet spins very slowly,

466
00:23:39,670 --> 00:23:43,530
其一侧要比另一侧热很多
one side is much hotter than the other.

467
00:23:43,640 --> 00:23:48,060
其岩质地面狂风大作 -
And its rocky surface is blasted by constant wind --

468
00:23:48,180 --> 00:23:51,940
是放风筝的好地方
a great place to fly a kite.

469
00:23:57,190 --> 00:24:00,440
生物力学专家迈克尔.拉巴贝拉
Biomechanics expert Michael Labarbera

470
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:03,750
认为格利泽581d上的稠密大气层
believes the thick atmosphere on Gliese 581 D

471
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:06,180
将其表面笼罩成一片漆黑
would shroud the surface in darkness,

472
00:24:06,300 --> 00:24:11,420
所以生命得攀升寻找亮光
so life would have to climb up in search of light.

473
00:24:11,530 --> 00:24:16,030
他猜想有风筝状的植物攀爬至乌云上方
He imagines kite-shaped plants that rise above the storm clouds

474
00:24:16,140 --> 00:24:19,600
来得到日常所需的太阳能
to get their daily dose of solar energy.

475
00:24:19,710 --> 00:24:22,400
这些风筝植物必须能够进入
These kite plants have to be able to get up

476
00:24:22,510 --> 00:24:24,930
大气层的较高地方
into the higher regions of the atmosphere

477
00:24:25,050 --> 00:24:26,670
以获得足够的采光
in order to get enough light,

478
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:28,240
它们这样做是为了
and the way they do that is

479
00:24:28,350 --> 00:24:31,540
利用大气层中的切变
to utilize the shear in the atmosphere.

480
00:24:31,650 --> 00:24:34,440
迈克尔的风筝需要两股力量
Michael's kite requires two forces

481
00:24:34,560 --> 00:24:36,250
停留在高空并保持稳定 -
to stay aloft and stable --

482
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:38,710
有风升起风筝
wind to lift the kite,

483
00:24:38,830 --> 00:24:42,820
有锚使其避免一直往上升
and an anchor to keep it from blowing ever upward.

484
00:24:42,930 --> 00:24:46,220
外星风筝植物的动作差不多是同样的道理
The alien kite plant works much the same way.

485
00:24:46,340 --> 00:24:50,160
所以 我们假设这种特殊的植物
So,what we've posited for this particular plant is

486
00:24:50,270 --> 00:24:52,760
上升的一面在绳索的一头
a lifting surface on one end of the string,

487
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:54,960
而在绳索的另一头
and at the other end of the string,

488
00:24:55,080 --> 00:24:57,900
有东西具有降落伞的功能
something that functions like a parachute

489
00:24:58,010 --> 00:25:00,300
来产生拽力
that produces a drag force.

490
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:02,610
因为风随着海拔发生变化
And because the wind changes with altitude,

491
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:05,140
它们的行动表现出不同的速度
they're moving at different speeds.

492
00:25:05,250 --> 00:25:08,510
于是你有了升力来保持风筝的上升
You then get a lift force that keeps the kite up

493
00:25:08,620 --> 00:25:10,380
它被拽拉在减速伞上
and it pulls on the drag chute,

494
00:25:10,490 --> 00:25:12,750
但它保持着绳索的拉力
but that keeps the tension on the string

495
00:25:12,860 --> 00:25:15,320
整个系统是稳定的
and the whole system is stable.

496
00:25:15,430 --> 00:25:17,450
听起来不大可能？
Sounds unlikely?

497
00:25:17,570 --> 00:25:19,790
迈克尔却并不如此认为
Michael doesn't think so.

498
00:25:19,900 --> 00:25:22,860
多年来对地球生物的研究使他相信
Years of studying organisms on Earth has convinced him

499
00:25:22,970 --> 00:25:27,460
生命会进化成适应各种环境
that life would evolve to suit any environment.

500
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:30,500
进化会通过很奇怪的途径
Evolution goes through very strange pathways

501
00:25:30,610 --> 00:25:31,970
来达到目的地的
to get to an endpoint.

502
00:25:32,080 --> 00:25:33,800
这个特殊的对象
This particular one,

503
00:25:33,920 --> 00:25:36,910
我们在地球上并没有例子
we don't have an example here on Earth,

504
00:25:37,020 --> 00:25:40,680
但是在假设在地球这里
but on the planet posited here

505
00:25:40,790 --> 00:25:43,410
低于地平面的太阳辐射输入
with low solar input for the ground level

506
00:25:43,530 --> 00:25:44,880
和高风切变的星球上
and a high wind shear,

507
00:25:44,990 --> 00:25:47,550
那它就完全有可能起作用的
it's entirely possible that it could function.

508
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:51,150
靠近格利泽581d表面
Closer to the surface of Gliese 581D,

509
00:25:51,270 --> 00:25:54,360
曾经明亮的阳光黯淡了下去
the once bright sunlight dims

510
00:25:54,470 --> 00:25:57,860
此时这颗日外行星沉浸在了永恒的朦胧暮色之中
as this ex oplanet enters a permanent,hazy twilight.

511
00:25:57,970 --> 00:26:00,700
大气稠密昏暗
The atmosphere is thick and murky,

512
00:26:00,810 --> 00:26:02,930
然而温度足以维持生命
but warm enough to sustain life.

513
00:26:03,050 --> 00:26:06,240
事实上 迈克尔.拉巴贝拉推测
ln fact,Michael Labarbera speculates

514
00:26:06,350 --> 00:26:08,700
它可能拥有一个欣欣向荣的生态系统
that it could host a thriving ecosystem

515
00:26:08,820 --> 00:26:11,540
有猎手与猎物
of hunters and prey.

516
00:26:11,650 --> 00:26:14,580
有何种猎食者会在此进化呢？
What kind of predator would evolve here?

517
00:26:14,690 --> 00:26:18,880
空中猎手 - 薄薄的翅膀 蝙蝠状
An aerial hunter -- thin-winged and bat-like,

518
00:26:18,990 --> 00:26:23,850
却能够像信天翁那样翱翔数日
but able to soar and glide for days like an albatross.

519
00:26:23,970 --> 00:26:26,520
..蝙蝠-天翁？
...a bat-atross?

520
00:26:26,640 --> 00:26:28,020
这种动物
Now,this animal,

521
00:26:28,140 --> 00:26:31,160
因为大气层相对浑浊
because the atmosphere is relatively opaque,

522
00:26:31,270 --> 00:26:34,500
就必须要能够以最小的付出来长途跋涉
has to be able to travel long distances at minimal cost

523
00:26:34,610 --> 00:26:36,200
以找到它们的猎物
in order to find their prey.

524
00:26:36,310 --> 00:26:37,600
它就有了长长的翅膀
lt's got long wings.

525
00:26:37,710 --> 00:26:41,570
它就有了相对较窄的翅膀 因为更具有效率
lt's got relatively narrow wings because they're more efficient.

526
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:44,740
